            <ArticleTitle>Prominent metalware from pelvic surgery causing dyspareunia.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">bcr2016217285</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1136/bcr-2016-217285</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We present a case of female dyspareunia secondary to metalware placement during extensive pelvic surgery following a motor vehicle accident. The patient initially had an uneventful recovery from her operations. However, she noticed pain with vaginal intercourse, due to a screw tip which was palpable on vaginal examination. X-ray imaging confirmed long screws in the medial part of an anterior column plate, which were impacting on the anterior vaginal wall. Subsequent percutaneous removal of two screws resulted in resolution of her symptoms of painful vaginal intercourse. While the pain from mechanical irritation of the vagina was resolved, the patient continues to have difficulty with intercourse, which is related to hip pain as a result of her initial injury and complex pelvic surgery.</AbstractText>
